[Migrated from birth.notes]: Born in the house on Main St., Durham. [Birth has_discrepancy flag set]: See research notes for resolution. [Migrated from marriage.notes (spouse I00083)]: Eunice Johnson was 19 at marriage; they made their home with father Abner. [Migrated from top-level anecdotes]: {"born_without_a_roof_to_his_mouth": "Burwell Newton was born with no roof to his mouth \u2014 a cleft palate. When he began school in the little schoolhouse on Phillida Hill, some of the children laughed at his attempts at pronunciation, and he refused to attend. He learned to read, write, and keep accounts properly, but his spelling was \"decidedly phonetic.\" As Caroline Newton put it: \"If he chose to spell his principal crop 'ri,' that is the best way to spell it.\" His carefully kept account book, dated January 1st, 1762, survived and was published in Caroline Newton's history. His cleft palate also complicated his Revolutionary War service \u2014 strangers could not understand him, and he was liable to be shot when unable to give the countersign intelligibly.", "the_colonial_militia_exemption": "When Great Britain declared war with France, the Colonial militia were liable to be called to Canada to fight for the mother country. Burwell's inability to give the countersign intelligibly was a serious military problem. His Captain consulted a higher officer, who granted an exemption. The document of exemption was preserved in the family."}
