[Stage 6Y-264 NB#2 header trim]: 'Defended Kingston and the Continental Congress in 1777 — called back to Connecticut in 1779' → 'Defended Kingston and the Continental Congress in 1777'. NSDAR No. 99934, Miss Sarah Newton Church; DAR Lineage Book 100:291. THE PRINTED GENEALOGY NAMES EXACTLY EIGHT CHILDREN — Abiathar, Burwell, Mary, Submit, Abner and Roger (twins), Roger and Isaac. Eunice (HD0165), Betsey (HD0167) and Nancy (HD0168) are NOT among them and are almost certainly not his; they are left attached pending an instruction to delete. EUNICE (HD0165) and BETSEY (HD0167) DELETED on Sam's instruction. Both were empty shells — no dates, no marriage, no burial, no narrative, no cross-connections, and no inbound references anywhere in the tree except this record and their mother's. The only thing either carried was the source line 'Caroline Gaylord Newton 1914' and the durham_newton tag, so nothing in them pointed to another family. The printed genealogy names exactly eight children here and neither was among them. NANCY (HD0168) is identical in every respect and is still attached, held back only because this instruction named two. Nancy (HD0168) deleted on Sam's instruction, the third of the three empty shells, on the same evidence as Eunice and Betsey.

b. Mar 1, 1674 Wethersfield CT; d. Nov 12, 1727 Glastonbury CT age 53. Son of Capt. Samuel Talcott (T00036) and Hannah Holyoke (U00025). Talcott Pedigree #135. m. (1) Sarah Hollister Jan 5, 1699 — 8 children. Sarah died in childbed Oct 15, 1715: "in ye 39 year of her age" (gravestone). Buried Green Cemetery, Glastonbury. Gravestone: "HERE LYES THE BODY OF SARAH, WIFE OF LEUT. TALLCOTT, WHO DIED OCT. 15, 1715." m. (2) Second wife [name unknown] — no issue. Also held rank of Lieutenant in the Connecticut Trained Band. Settled Glastonbury 1699: built house on farm purchased by his father from Samuel Sherman and Richard Gildersleeve in 1643. House "was fortified and used as a place of refuge from frequent attacks of the Indians" — still standing 1876, owned by Jared G. Talcott. Estate: £2563 15s. 8d. by inventory.
b. March 1, 1674 Wethersfield CT; d. Nov 12, 1727 Glastonbury CT aged 53. Wife: Sarah Hollister (daughter of John Hollister Jr. and Sarah Goodrich), married Jan 5, 1699. Sarah died Oct 15, 1715 in childbirth, aged 34. He remarried (second wife name unknown, no issue). Built fortified house on Main Street Glastonbury 1699 — refuge from Indian attacks. Settled on farm "purchased of Mr. Samuel Sherman and Mr. Richard Gildersleeve in the year 1643" — farm had been in the family since before his birth. NOTE: Sarah Hollister's grandfather was John Hollister the emigrant (Y00006 in our JSON) who came to Hartford with Thomas Hooker in 1636. John Hollister Jr. married Sarah Goodrich Nov 20, 1667. SOURCE: Talcott Pedigree 1876; screenshot of Hollister genealogy.
SONS AS BOLTON FOUNDERS: Sons T00455 Benjamin and T00437 Nathaniel listed as Bolton Proprietors in the 1731 patent, signed by Governor Joseph Talcott (T00045). SON T00459 Elizur chaired Glastonbury's denunciation of the Boston Port Bill. SON T00460 Mehitabel's husband Hezekiah Wright (U00050) was the first cousin of son T00459 Elizur's wife Ruth Wright (U00049). Two Wright cousins married two Talcott siblings. [Migrated from marriage.notes (spouse U00044)]: Referred to in his will but name and marriage date unrecorded. No issue by second wife. [Migrated from marriage.spouse_notes (spouse U00044)]: [Name unknown] [Migrated from top-level anecdotes]: {"the_glastonbury_homestead_two_hundred_years": "Built his house in 1699 (moved in Nov 22, 1699) upon the farm given by his father's will \u2014 the land purchased in 1643 by grandfather John Talcott of Samuel Sherman and Richard Gildersleeve. The house was a large wooden building on Main Street, fortified as a place of refuge from frequent Indian attacks. It bore the marks of bullets until it was taken down soon after 1854. In 1876 the farm was still owned by Jared G. Talcott, a descendant \u2014 over 200 years in the family.", "sarahs_death_in_childbed": "Sarah Hollister died in childbed at the homestead, October 15, 1715, aged 34 years, 11 months and 20 days."}

