b. Aug 8, 1808 Brooklyn NY; d. Mar 28, 1888 Brooklyn NY age 79. "First citizen of Brooklyn." Thomas Hooker descendant through Mary Hooker Pierpont (H00034). CITY PLANNING: In 1833 while in Europe, appointed to Board of Commissioners to lay out streets of the newly incorporated City of Brooklyn. His plan included 11 parks and designated the terminal glacial moraine above Gowanus Creek for what became Green-Wood Cemetery. Considered by some "one of the first city planners in the United States." GREEN-WOOD CEMETERY: Co-founder and President 1864. The cemetery's popularity directly led to the establishment of New York's Central Park in 1856. (A.J. Downing, 1849) Gothic Revival catafalque by Richard Upjohn marks family plot. BROOKLYN UNION FERRY: With Jacob R. Leroy, combined five existing ferries into the Brooklyn Union Ferry Company 1844 — monopolized East River crossings until the Brooklyn Bridge opened 1883. PIERREPONT STORES: Bonded warehouse 1856-57 on East River waterfront for sugar, molasses from Caribbean to Manila. AMERICAN GEOGRAPHICAL SOCIETY: Co-founder 1851-54; Council member 10 years. Also: first president Brooklyn Academy of Music; Trustee Brooklyn Hospital, Long Island Historical Society; "proprietor of immense landed estates in Northern New York, in which he had made more than 300 miles of road and executed more than 2,000 deeds." (Dwight) Pierrepont Street in Brooklyn Heights commemorates the family. SOURCES: Family papers (1761-1918) at Brooklyn Historical Society (ARC.263);
GILBERT STUART WASHINGTON PORTRAIT: HD1533 owned a full-length portrait of George Washington painted by Gilbert Stuart in 1796-97 — a duplicate of the celebrated Lansdowne portrait (painted for the Marquis of Lansdowne). His maternal grandfather William Constable (father of Anna Maria Constable, X00495) commissioned the duplicate while in Philadelphia for his own portrait sitting. Stuart painted both simultaneously while Washington was actively giving sittings. Stuart's receipted bill survives, dated July 1797. LAFAYETTE authentication: In 1824, Lafayette visited the Constable/Pierrepont household and said of the portrait: "That is really my old friend indeed." LEWIS MARSHALL authentication (1853 NYC exhibition): Lewis Marshall — brother of Chief Justice John Marshall, former neighbor of Washington in Virginia — passed every other portrait without comment and said of this one: "That is prodigiously like Washington." He later confirmed this in writing. HD1533 recounted this history in a letter to Massachusetts Historical Society President R.C. Winthrop, read at the March 1870 meeting. (Proceedings of the Massachusetts Historical Society, Vol. 11, 1869-1870)
CC NOTE [HD0867]: Henry Evelyn Pierrepont co-founded the American Geographical Society (1851) alongside Joshua Leavitt — abolitionist editor and Hooker descendant through the Leavitt line. Two Hooker descendants, through entirely separate branches, among the 31 founders of the same institution in 1851. Neither knew the shared ancestry.
